---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Exporting to your own Immich server — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightPhotos can upload finished photos straight to any Immich server, including one on a box in your closet. Here is what it took to make a custom server URL and an API key just work."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-26">September 26, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Exporting to your own Immich server</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          LightPhotos can upload finished photos straight to any Immich server,
          including one on a box in your closet. Here is what it took to make
          a custom server URL and an API key just work.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          Immich is a self-hosted photo library, the closest thing to Google
          Photos you can run yourself. The export form in LightPhotos now has
          an Immich tab: paste your server's address and an API key, press
          Connect, and exports go to your server instead of a folder. Each
          upload is the edited JPEG, with the capture time and star rating
          carried over.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of the work wasn't the upload. It was taking whatever address
          people type and finding the real API, checking the key up front,
          surviving slow home uploads, and not sending anyone's key over plain
          HTTP by accident. It's desktop only, because browsers can't talk to
          an Immich server.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Check a key with a request that needs it.</strong> One
          hosted Immich service answers its "about this server" call for any
          key at all, so a wrong key looked fine until the first upload
          failed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Let the server do the de-duplication.</strong> Immich
          already recognises a photo it has seen before. Keeping my own record
          of what was uploaded would only have been a second copy of the truth
          that could drift.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A timeout should grow with the work.</strong> One fixed
          five-minute limit failed large batches on healthy but slow
          connections.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A time without a time zone is a guess.</strong> Photos taken
          at 6&nbsp;pm in California showed up in Immich at 11&nbsp;am.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why Immich</h2>
      <p>
        Folders on a drive are how I keep my originals, and that won't change.
        But nobody wants to browse a folder of JPEGs on their phone, and I
        don't want to hand my photos to a service I can't leave. Immich is a
        good middle ground: a real photo library with a phone app, running on
        hardware I own, or on a hosted service if I'd rather not run it
        myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before this, getting photos there meant exporting to a folder and then
        uploading that folder by hand. Now it's one step.
      </p>

      <h2>Finding the API from whatever you type</h2>
      <p>
        Everyone's server lives somewhere different. Some people type
        <code>photos.example.com</code>, some paste
        <code>https://photos.example.com/api/</code> from the docs, some use
        <code>http://nas.local:2283</code> on their home network. All of those
        should work.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 214" role="img" aria-label="How LightPhotos connects. First it tidies the address: trims spaces, removes a trailing slash and /api, and adds https if there's no scheme. Then it asks the server's well-known discovery file where the API lives, and falls back to /api if there isn't one. Then it checks the key by asking who it belongs to. Only if that succeeds are the address and key saved.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">FROM A TYPED ADDRESS TO A CONNECTION</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="28" width="196" height="54" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="98" y="48" text-anchor="middle">1. Tidy the address</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="98" y="64" text-anchor="middle">trim, drop trailing / and /api,</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="98" y="76" text-anchor="middle">add https:// if missing</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="196" y1="55" x2="212" y2="55"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M212 50 L222 55 L212 60 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="222" y="28" width="196" height="54" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="48" text-anchor="middle">2. Ask where the API is</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="320" y="64" text-anchor="middle">/.well-known/immich</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="320" y="76" text-anchor="middle">otherwise assume /api</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="418" y1="55" x2="434" y2="55"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M434 50 L444 55 L434 60 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="444" y="28" width="196" height="54" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="542" y="48" text-anchor="middle">3. Check the key</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="542" y="64" text-anchor="middle">GET /users/me</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="542" y="76" text-anchor="middle">"Connected as …"</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="542" y1="82" x2="542" y2="104"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M537 104 L547 104 L542 114 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="444" y="114" width="196" height="40" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="542" y="138" text-anchor="middle">Save address and key</text>

          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="6" cy="176" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="18" y="180">Wrong key: "the server rejected the API key", nothing saved.</text>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="6" cy="200" r="3"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="18" y="204">No HTTPS: a hint to type http:// yourself, never a silent downgrade.</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <p>
        First the address gets tidied: spaces trimmed, a trailing slash or
        <code>/api</code> removed, and <code>https://</code> added if you left
        it off. Then LightPhotos asks the server's discovery file,
        <code>/.well-known/immich</code>, where its API lives. That covers
        people who host Immich under a sub-path like
        <code>/immich/api</code>. If there's no discovery file, it assumes the
        usual <code>/api</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you didn't type a scheme and HTTPS fails, it does not quietly try
        plain HTTP. That would send your API key across the network
        unencrypted without telling you. The error says to start the address
        with <code>http://</code> if your server really doesn't have HTTPS,
        and then it's your call.
      </p>

      <h2>Checking the key before it matters</h2>
      <p>
        The obvious way to test a connection is to ask the server about
        itself. I tried that against Gumnut, a hosted Immich service, and it
        answered for any key, including a made-up one. So instead LightPhotos
        asks "who does this key belong to?" That only works with a real key,
        and it gives the form something nice to show: "Connected as" your
        name and email.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing is saved until that check passes. Then the address goes into
        the app's settings, and the key goes somewhere safer.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>On a Mac</strong>, the key goes into your login Keychain,
          with one entry per server.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On Linux</strong>, it's a file in the app's config folder
          that only your user can read.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>On Windows</strong>, it's a file in your own app data
          folder.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        One small detail I'm glad I got right: the Keychain isn't touched
        until you open the Immich tab. If you never use Immich, you never see
        a Keychain prompt.
      </p>

      <h2>What actually gets uploaded</h2>
      <p>
        Immich gets the finished photo, not the original. LightPhotos renders
        it exactly as it would for a folder export, with your edits, crop and
        size, and encodes a JPEG. Your originals stay on your drive, where
        they belong.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two pieces of information come along with it:
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>When it was taken.</strong> The first version read the
          camera's clock as if it were UTC, so a photo taken at 6&nbsp;pm in
          California appeared in Immich at 11&nbsp;am. Now each export carries
          its capture time and the camera's time-zone offset, and uses the
          file's date only when the camera didn't record one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your star rating,</strong> set right after the upload. Some
          keys are allowed to upload but not to change photos. In that case
          the photo still uploads and you get a warning about the rating,
          rather than a failed export.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Running the same batch twice</h2>
      <p>
        Interrupted exports happen: the laptop sleeps, the Wi-Fi drops. The
        simplest thing is to run the export again, so that has to be safe.
      </p>
      <p>
        Immich already handles this. Upload the same bytes twice and it
        replies "duplicate" with the photo it already has. LightPhotos keeps
        no list of what it has uploaded. It just re-sends, counts the
        duplicates, and tells you: "Uploaded 40 photos, 32 already on the
        server." The batch finishes where it left off without me writing any
        bookkeeping.
      </p>

      <h2>Slow uploads that aren't broken</h2>
      <p>
        Home internet uploads slowly, and LightPhotos exports on several
        threads at once. The first version gave each upload a fixed
        five-minute limit. A batch of large photos on a slow connection shared
        eight ways would hit that limit while the connection was perfectly
        healthy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now each upload gets a minute plus time for its size, worked out as
        if the connection were a 1&nbsp;Mbit/s link split between eight
        uploads:
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">time allowed = 60 s + photo size / 16 KB per second</pre>
      <p>
        A 25&nbsp;MB photo gets about 28 minutes. That sounds generous, but a
        stalled connection still fails, because the other limits (connecting,
        waiting for the server's reply) stay short.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it isn't in the browser</h2>
      <p>
        The web version of LightPhotos shows the Immich tab with a note that
        it only works in the desktop app. A web page can only talk to another
        server if that server explicitly allows it, and Immich doesn't. The
        browser blocks the request before it leaves. The desktop app isn't a
        web page, so the rule doesn't apply.
      </p>

      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <p>
        I'm working on album support: pick an existing album or name a new
        one, and the batch goes into it. After that, automatic retries for
        uploads that fail partway through. Servers with self-signed
        certificates, common on home networks, probably won't connect yet.
        If you run one, I'd like to hear whether it works.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you run Immich and something about this doesn't fit your setup,
        tell me on the <a
        href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">issue
        tracker</a>. The desktop builds are on the <a
        href="/downloads.html">downloads page</a>.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/challenges-with-webassembly.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Challenges with WebAssembly</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
